# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "[*Hellinger fidelity*](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/quantum_info#hellinger_fidelity) between two probability distributions $p$ and $q$ on $n$-bit strings:\n"
msgstr "[*Hellinger fidelity*](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/quantum_info#hellinger_fidelity) antara dua distribusi probabilitas $p$ dan $q$ pada string $n$-bit:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "    $$F(p, q) = \\bigg(\\sum_i \\sqrt{p_i\\, q_i}\\,\\bigg)^2,$$\n"
msgstr "    $$F(p, q) = \\bigg(\\sum_i \\sqrt{p_i\\, q_i}\\,\\bigg)^2,$$\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  where the sum runs over all $n$-bit strings $i$, and $p_i$ and $q_i$ are the probabilities that $p$ and $q$ assign to $i$. $F = 1$ for identical distributions and $F = 0$ when no bitstring is assigned positive probability by both distributions. $F$ is a classical distribution-level quantity computed *after* measurement and does not certify the full quantum state; for example, a GHZ state and its phase-flipped counterpart can produce identical computational-basis distributions and therefore identical $F$. We return to this potential obstacle when motivating stabilizer-code checks in [Section 1.2](#stabilizer-code).\n"
"- *Detection region* of a check: the set of circuit locations at which a fault can flip that check; which Pauli types are caught at each location depends on the check. The final checks of Part 1 can only respond to faults whose effects survive to the final measurements, and whether a surviving fault is actually detected depends on the particular check. Part 2 places checks whose regions are deliberately narrower and traces them through the circuit.\n"
msgstr ""
"  dengan jumlah dijalankan atas semua string $n$-bit $i$, dan $p_i$ serta $q_i$ adalah probabilitas yang diberikan $p$ dan $q$ kepada $i$. $F = 1$ untuk distribusi yang identik dan $F = 0$ ketika tidak ada bitstring yang diberi probabilitas positif oleh kedua distribusi. $F$ adalah besaran klasik tingkat distribusi yang dihitung *setelah* pengukuran dan tidak menjamin keseluruhan state kuantum; misalnya, state GHZ dan pasangannya yang phase-flipped bisa menghasilkan distribusi basis komputasi yang identik sehingga $F$-nya pun identik. Kita kembali ke kendala potensial ini saat memotivasi check stabilizer-code di [Bagian 1.2](#stabilizer-code).\n"
"- *Detection region* dari sebuah check: himpunan lokasi dalam Circuit di mana sebuah fault bisa membalik check itu; jenis Pauli yang tertangkap di setiap lokasi bergantung pada check-nya. Check akhir pada Bagian 1 hanya bisa merespons fault yang efeknya bertahan sampai pengukuran akhir, dan apakah fault yang bertahan itu benar-benar terdeteksi bergantung pada check tertentu. Bagian 2 menempatkan check yang detection region-nya sengaja dibuat lebih sempit dan menelusurinya di sepanjang Circuit.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "1.1.1 GHZ as a benchmark for symmetry filtering {#111-ghz-as-a-benchmark-for-symmetry-filtering}"
msgstr "1.1.1 GHZ sebagai benchmark untuk symmetry filtering {#111-ghz-as-a-benchmark-for-symmetry-filtering}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"We use the $n$-qubit GHZ state\n"
"$$\n"
"|\\mathrm{GHZ}_n\\rangle = \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\big(|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n} + |1\\rangle^{\\otimes n}\\big).\n"
"$$\n"
"In the computational basis, the ideal outcome distribution is supported on exactly two bitstrings, $0^n$ and $1^n$. Any other bitstring can be rejected in classical post-processing as inconsistent with this support.\n"
msgstr ""
"Kita memakai state GHZ $n$-qubit\n"
"$$\n"
"|\\mathrm{GHZ}_n\\rangle = \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\big(|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n} + |1\\rangle^{\\otimes n}\\big).\n"
"$$\n"
"Di basis komputasi, distribusi hasil ideal hanya mendukung tepat dua bitstring, $0^n$ dan $1^n$. Bitstring lain mana pun bisa ditolak dalam post-processing klasik karena tidak konsisten dengan dukungan ini.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For our run we use $n = 10$. Both ideal outputs $0^{10}$ and $1^{10}$ have an even number of `1`s (0 and 10, respectively), so we compare two rules on the same dataset:\n"
msgstr "Untuk eksekusi kita, kita pakai $n = 10$. Kedua output ideal $0^{10}$ dan $1^{10}$ punya jumlah `1` yang genap (masing-masing 0 dan 10), jadi kita bandingkan dua aturan pada dataset yang sama:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*Even-parity rule.* Keep shots whose number of `1`s is even. This rule applies only when $n$ is even (the case here); for odd $n$ the two ideal outputs have opposite parity, and no single parity rule keeps both.\n"
msgstr "*Aturan paritas genap.* Simpan shot yang jumlah `1`-nya genap. Aturan ini hanya berlaku ketika $n$ genap (kasus di sini); untuk $n$ ganjil, kedua output ideal punya paritas yang berlawanan, dan tidak ada satu aturan paritas pun yang menyimpan keduanya.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*All-agree rule.* Keep shots that are exactly $0^n$ or $1^n$.\n"
msgstr "*Aturan all-agree.* Simpan shot yang persis $0^n$ atau $1^n$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The all-agree rule is more restrictive than even parity: every all-agree bitstring has an even number of `1`s, but most bitstrings with an even number of `1`s are not all-agree. Even parity catches any odd number of bit flips but lets through any pair of compensating flips. Under noise, we therefore expect the all-agree rule to give higher post-selected fidelity at a lower post-selection rate.\n"
msgstr "Aturan all-agree lebih ketat daripada paritas genap: setiap bitstring all-agree punya jumlah `1` yang genap, tetapi sebagian besar bitstring dengan jumlah `1` genap bukan all-agree. Paritas genap menangkap bit flip berjumlah ganjil mana pun, tetapi meloloskan setiap pasangan flip yang saling mengimbangi. Karena itu, di bawah noise, kita mengharapkan aturan all-agree memberi fidelity pasca-seleksi yang lebih tinggi dengan post-selection rate yang lebih rendah.\n"

