msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i/extracted-outputs/a446654c-1.avif)\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the saved run, post-selection improves the measured Hellinger fidelity, with the highest value obtained when both syndromes pass. Requiring both checks also gives the lowest acceptance, so the improvement comes at the cost of fewer usable shots. This measures agreement of the final Z-basis distribution with the ideal distribution, rather than certifying the full quantum state. On another backend or calibration, compare the fidelity and acceptance again. Requiring both syndromes always keeps a subset of the shots accepted by either check alone, but the fidelity ordering can change.\n"
msgstr "Pada eksekusi yang disimpan, post-selection meningkatkan Hellinger fidelity terukur, dengan nilai tertinggi diperoleh ketika kedua syndrome lolos. Mensyaratkan kedua check juga memberi acceptance terendah, jadi peningkatan itu datang dengan harga berkurangnya shot yang bisa dipakai. Ini mengukur kesesuaian distribusi basis Z akhir dengan distribusi ideal, bukan menjamin keseluruhan state kuantum. Pada Backend atau kalibrasi lain, bandingkan lagi fidelity dan acceptance-nya. Mensyaratkan kedua syndrome selalu menyimpan himpunan bagian dari shot yang diterima oleh salah satu check saja, tetapi urutan fidelity-nya bisa berubah.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-2}"
msgstr "Uji pemahamanmu {#check-your-understanding-2}"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Suppose $X$ errors strike both qubit $0$ and qubit $1$ simultaneously ($X_0 X_1$). What syndrome would $Z_3Z_2Z_1Z_0$ produce? Would $X_3X_2X_1X_0$ catch it? Would the error be detected at all?\n"
msgstr "Misalkan error $X$ menimpa qubit $0$ dan qubit $1$ secara bersamaan ($X_0 X_1$). Syndrome apa yang akan dihasilkan $Z_3Z_2Z_1Z_0$? Apakah $X_3X_2X_1X_0$ akan menangkapnya? Apakah error itu terdeteksi sama sekali?\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For $Z_3Z_2Z_1Z_0$: each $X$ flips one qubit's contribution to the $Z$-eigenvalue, and two flips cancel, so $Z_3Z_2Z_1Z_0$ returns $+1$ (syndrome bit `0`). For $X_3X_2X_1X_0$: $X_0 X_1$ commutes with $X_3X_2X_1X_0$ (both are products of $X$s), so $X_3X_2X_1X_0$ also returns $+1$. The error goes undetected. This is the $d=2$ shortcoming: $X_0 X_1$ is a weight-$2$ logical operator that maps one valid codeword to another without leaving the codespace.\n"
msgstr "Untuk $Z_3Z_2Z_1Z_0$: setiap $X$ membalik kontribusi satu qubit pada eigenvalue $Z$, dan dua flip saling meniadakan, sehingga $Z_3Z_2Z_1Z_0$ mengembalikan $+1$ (bit syndrome `0`). Untuk $X_3X_2X_1X_0$: $X_0 X_1$ komutatif dengan $X_3X_2X_1X_0$ (keduanya adalah hasil kali $X$), jadi $X_3X_2X_1X_0$ juga mengembalikan $+1$. Error itu tidak terdeteksi. Inilah kekurangan $d=2$: $X_0 X_1$ adalah operator logis berbobot-$2$ yang memetakan satu codeword valid ke codeword valid lain tanpa meninggalkan codespace.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-27
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"Consider two errors: $X_0$ (single bit-flip on qubit $0$) and $X_1$ (single bit-flip on qubit $1$). Both are weight-$1$ errors that the $[[4,2,2]]$ code does detect. Can the code also tell them apart, that is, can it identify *which* qubit was hit?\n"
msgstr ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"Perhatikan dua error: $X_0$ (bit-flip tunggal pada qubit $0$) dan $X_1$ (bit-flip tunggal pada qubit $1$). Keduanya adalah error berbobot-$1$ yang memang terdeteksi oleh kode $[[4,2,2]]$. Apakah kode itu juga bisa membedakan keduanya, yaitu apakah ia bisa mengidentifikasi qubit *mana* yang terkena?\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "No. Both $X_0$ and $X_1$ produce the same syndrome, $s_Z = 1$ and $s_X = 0$. The two-bit syndrome identifies that an $X$-type error happened, but not *which* qubit was affected. Equivalently, $X_0$ and $X_1$ differ by the logical operator $\\bar X_1 = X_0 X_1$, and logical operators are invisible to stabilizer measurements, so $X_0$ and $X_1 = X_0 \\cdot (X_0 X_1)$ look identical to the syndrome.\n"
msgstr "Tidak. Baik $X_0$ maupun $X_1$ menghasilkan syndrome yang sama, $s_Z = 1$ dan $s_X = 0$. Syndrome dua-bit mengidentifikasi bahwa error tipe-$X$ terjadi, tetapi bukan qubit *mana* yang terkena. Dengan kata lain, $X_0$ dan $X_1$ berbeda sebesar operator logis $\\bar X_1 = X_0 X_1$, dan operator logis tidak terlihat oleh pengukuran stabilizer, jadi $X_0$ dan $X_1 = X_0 \\cdot (X_0 X_1)$ tampak identik bagi syndrome.\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This is why we use the $[[4,2,2]]$ code for detection only: it cannot correct an arbitrary single-qubit error. Codes with $d \\geq 3$, such as the Steane $[[7,1,3]]$ code, can correct any single-qubit error with ideal syndrome extraction.\n"
msgstr "Inilah sebabnya kita memakai kode $[[4,2,2]]$ hanya untuk deteksi: kode ini tidak bisa mengoreksi error single-qubit sembarang. Kode dengan $d \\geq 3$, seperti kode Steane $[[7,1,3]]$, bisa mengoreksi error single-qubit mana pun dengan ekstraksi syndrome yang ideal.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"The post-selection filter keeps shots where both syndrome bits read $0$, but passing the filter is not the same as correctness: some accepted shots can still land outside the ideal support of $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$. The cell below extracts these *false-accept* shots from the hardware data and breaks them into two categories: those whose data bitstrings have even parity but lie outside the target support, and those with odd parity. These are categories of computational-basis outcomes, not a test of whether the quantum state was in the codespace.\n"
msgstr ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"Filter post-selection menyimpan shot yang kedua bit syndrome-nya bernilai $0$, tetapi lolos filter tidak sama dengan benar: sebagian shot yang diterima masih bisa jatuh di luar dukungan ideal $|\\Phi^+\\rangle_L$. Sel di bawah mengekstrak shot *false-accept* ini dari data hardware dan membaginya menjadi dua kategori: yang bitstring datanya berparitas genap tetapi berada di luar dukungan target, dan yang berparitas ganjil. Ini adalah kategori hasil basis komputasi, bukan uji apakah state kuantumnya berada di codespace.\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The false-accept shots could have arisen from several mechanisms: readout errors on the data or ancilla qubits, gate errors in the syndrome circuit, weight-$2$ errors that the syndrome cannot detect, or non-Pauli errors such as energy relaxation, which a stabilizer measurement catches only with some probability. The \"closest ideal(s)\" column compares bitstrings, but it does not identify which of these mechanisms occurred or certify that the quantum state was in the codespace. [Section 1.3](#limitations) takes up these limits.\n"
msgstr "Shot false-accept bisa muncul dari beberapa mekanisme: error readout pada qubit data atau ancilla, error Gate pada Circuit syndrome, error berbobot-$2$ yang tidak bisa dideteksi syndrome, atau error non-Pauli seperti relaksasi energi, yang hanya tertangkap oleh pengukuran stabilizer dengan probabilitas tertentu. Kolom \"closest ideal(s)\" membandingkan bitstring, tetapi tidak mengidentifikasi mekanisme mana yang terjadi dan tidak menjamin bahwa state kuantum berada di codespace. [Bagian 1.3](#limitations) membahas batasan-batasan ini.\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Readout error mitigation techniques such as M3 or TREX could be applied to reduce the readout contribution and obtain higher-fidelity post-selected results.\n"
msgstr "Teknik mitigasi error readout seperti M3 atau TREX bisa diterapkan untuk mengurangi kontribusi readout dan mendapatkan hasil pasca-seleksi dengan fidelity lebih tinggi.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-3}"
msgstr "Uji pemahamanmu {#check-your-understanding-3}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A stabilizer code has parameters $[[n, k, d]]$ with $r = n - k$ stabilizer generators. Suppose we want to encode more logical qubits at the same distance. Must the number of stabilizer generators increase, and what determines the syndrome-extraction cost?\n"
msgstr "Sebuah stabilizer code memiliki parameter $[[n, k, d]]$ dengan $r = n - k$ stabilizer generator. Misalkan kita ingin mengenkode lebih banyak qubit logis pada distance yang sama. Apakah jumlah stabilizer generator harus bertambah, dan apa yang menentukan biaya ekstraksi syndrome?\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Not necessarily: $r = n - k$ need not increase with $k$. For example, even-$n$ codes with $n \\geq 4$ and generators $X^{\\otimes n}$ and $Z^{\\otimes n}$ have parameters $[[n,n-2,2]]$: they keep two generators while their weights grow. Syndrome-extraction cost depends on the number and weights of the generators, routing, and whether ancillas are reused. Two-qubit gates are typically a major noise source on near-term hardware, so a larger extraction circuit can introduce more noise and reduce the post-selection rate.\n"
msgstr "Belum tentu: $r = n - k$ tidak harus naik seiring $k$. Misalnya, kode dengan $n$ genap dan $n \\geq 4$ serta generator $X^{\\otimes n}$ dan $Z^{\\otimes n}$ punya parameter $[[n,n-2,2]]$: kode ini tetap punya dua generator meskipun bobotnya bertambah. Biaya ekstraksi syndrome bergantung pada jumlah dan bobot generator, routing, serta apakah ancilla dipakai ulang. Gate dua-qubit biasanya jadi sumber noise utama di hardware near-term, jadi Circuit ekstraksi yang lebih besar bisa menambah noise dan menurunkan post-selection rate.\n"

