# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.3.1 Anti-commutation rule {#231-anti-commutation-rule}"
msgstr "2.3.1 Aturan anti-komutasi {#231-anti-commutation-rule}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A CPC check detects an error if and only if the error *anti-commutes* with the check operator. For an error injected between $U_A$ and $U_B$, there are two equivalent ways to see it:\n"
msgstr "Check CPC mendeteksi error jika dan hanya jika error itu *anti-komut* dengan operator check. Untuk error yang disuntikkan di antara $U_A$ dan $U_B$, ada dua cara yang setara untuk melihatnya:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*From $R$'s side:* the error propagates forward through $U_B$, arriving at $R$ as $U_B E U_B^\\dagger$. Detection depends on whether this commutes with $R$.\n"
msgstr "*Dari sisi $R$:* error merambat maju melalui $U_B$, tiba di $R$ sebagai $U_B E U_B^\\dagger$. Deteksi bergantung pada apakah ini komut dengan $R$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "*From $L$'s side:* the error propagates backward through $U_A^\\dagger$, arriving at $L$ as $U_A^\\dagger E U_A$. Detection depends on whether this commutes with $L$.\n"
msgstr "*Dari sisi $L$:* error merambat mundur melalui $U_A^\\dagger$, tiba di $L$ sebagai $U_A^\\dagger E U_A$. Deteksi bergantung pada apakah ini komut dengan $L$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Since $L = U^\\dagger R U$, these two conditions are algebraically equivalent: $L$ and $R$ always agree. We verify this:\n"
msgstr "Karena $L = U^\\dagger R U$, kedua kondisi ini setara secara aljabar: $L$ dan $R$ selalu sepakat. Kita verifikasi ini:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$L$ and $R$ always agree on each error: what one detects, the other detects too. Whether a given error is caught is not simply a matter of distance from the check's support: the rule is anti-commutation between the check operator and the error at the location it is evaluated. Some errors near the support are caught, others commute with the check there and pass. Errors far from the support often propagate through too few gates to reach $R$ as a non-trivial operator, and so they pass as well.\n"
msgstr "$L$ dan $R$ selalu sepakat pada tiap error: apa yang dideteksi satu, dideteksi juga oleh yang lain. Apakah suatu error tertangkap bukan sekadar soal jarak dari support check: aturannya adalah anti-komutasi antara operator check dan error di lokasi tempat ia dievaluasi. Beberapa error di dekat support tertangkap, yang lain komut dengan check di sana dan lolos. Error yang jauh dari support sering merambat melalui terlalu sedikit gate untuk sampai ke $R$ sebagai operator non-trivial, sehingga mereka juga lolos.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we quantify this: each of the 20 qubits can suffer an $X$, $Y$, or $Z$ error, giving $20 \\times 3 = 60$ possible single-qubit errors at the mid-circuit location. How many does this single CPC pair catch?\n"
msgstr "Sekarang kita kuantifikasi: tiap dari 20 qubit bisa mengalami error $X$, $Y$, atau $Z$, sehingga ada $20 \\times 3 = 60$ kemungkinan error satu-qubit di lokasi mid-circuit. Berapa banyak yang tertangkap oleh satu pasangan CPC ini?\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The detected fraction depends on the check's support at the error location. If the propagated check acts on $w$ of the $n$ data qubits there, it anti-commutes with two of $\\{X,Y,Z\\}$ on each supported qubit and commutes with all three on the others. This sweep therefore detects $2w$ of the $3n$ single-qubit Pauli faults, a fraction $2w/(3n)$. Read the support and detected count from the output above; they change with the payload and the chosen insertion point. The support at this slice is part of the check's detection region, the spacetime wires reached by its back-cumulant ([Martiel & Javadi-Abhari, Supp. III-A](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725)).\n"
msgstr "Fraksi yang terdeteksi bergantung pada support check di lokasi error. Jika check yang dirambatkan bekerja pada $w$ dari $n$ qubit data di sana, ia anti-komut dengan dua dari $\\{X,Y,Z\\}$ pada tiap qubit yang didukung dan komut dengan ketiganya pada qubit lain. Sapuan ini karenanya mendeteksi $2w$ dari $3n$ error Pauli satu-qubit, yaitu fraksi $2w/(3n)$. Baca support dan jumlah terdeteksi dari output di atas; nilainya berubah sesuai payload dan titik penyisipan yang dipilih. Support pada irisan ini adalah bagian dari detection region check, yaitu wire spacetime yang dijangkau oleh back-cumulant-nya ([Martiel & Javadi-Abhari, Supp. III-A](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725)).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Checks with larger support at this slice catch a larger fraction of this sweep. Additional check pairs at different positions can extend coverage, but each brings its own $L$ operator. The detection rule as stated covers Pauli errors; a general error is a combination of Paulis, and a coherent or non-unitary error is caught only with the probability carried by the components that anti-commute with the check.\n"
msgstr "Check dengan support lebih besar pada irisan ini menangkap fraksi sapuan yang lebih besar. Pasangan check tambahan di posisi berbeda bisa memperluas cakupan, tapi masing-masing membawa operator $L$-nya sendiri. Aturan deteksi seperti yang dinyatakan mencakup error Pauli; error umum adalah kombinasi Pauli, dan error koheren atau non-unitari hanya tertangkap dengan probabilitas yang dibawa oleh komponen yang anti-komut dengan check.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.3.2 One-sided CPC: reading $R$ from the data {#232-one-sided-cpc-reading-r-from-the-data}"
msgstr "2.3.2 CPC satu sisi: membaca $R$ dari data {#232-one-sided-cpc-reading-r-from-the-data}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The compiled resource counts in Section 2.2.1 show why a valid CPC can still be expensive: one ancilla must reach the supports of both $L$ and $R$. Their weights count the controlled-Pauli couplings, while hardware connectivity and routing determine the additional cost. A lower-weight check need not give a shallower compiled circuit.\n"
msgstr "Jumlah sumber daya hasil kompilasi di Bagian 2.2.1 menunjukkan mengapa CPC yang valid tetap bisa mahal: satu ancilla harus menjangkau support $L$ dan $R$. Bobot keduanya menghitung kopling controlled-Pauli, sedangkan konektivitas hardware dan routing menentukan biaya tambahannya. Check berbobot lebih rendah belum tentu menghasilkan Circuit terkompilasi yang lebih dangkal.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In Section 2.5 of their [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03937), van den Berg et al. reduce that cost by moving the check qubit along a line and combining each controlled-Pauli gate with the following swap. Their construction shows why designing checks together with qubit motion can be cheaper than relying on a generic router. Their one-sided construction also removes controlled-$R$ gates by evaluating a Z-type $R$ from the final data bits; we illustrate that option below.\n"
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Implementation detail: phase compensation\">\n"
msgstr ""
"Di Bagian 2.5 [makalah](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03937) mereka, van den Berg et al. mengurangi biaya itu dengan menggerakkan qubit check sepanjang garis dan menggabungkan tiap gate controlled-Pauli dengan swap berikutnya. Konstruksi mereka menunjukkan mengapa merancang check bersama dengan gerakan qubit bisa lebih murah daripada mengandalkan router generik. Konstruksi satu sisi mereka juga menghilangkan gate controlled-$R$ dengan mengevaluasi $R$ bertipe-Z dari bit data akhir; kita ilustrasikan opsi itu di bawah.\n"
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Detail implementasi: kompensasi fase\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "`Pauli.phase` from Qiskit stores an overall factor $(-i)^k$. The `apply_controlled_paulis` helper implements the tensor factors but omits that phase. Under control it becomes a relative phase on the ancilla, so we restore it with $S^\\dagger$, $Z$, or $S$ for exponents 1, 2, or 3. For a CPC pair, use `L.phase + R.phase` modulo four. The one-sided example below uses `L.phase` alone. See [Martiel & Javadi-Abhari, Supplementary Information §II-C and Fig. S3](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725).\n"
msgstr "`Pauli.phase` dari Qiskit menyimpan faktor keseluruhan $(-i)^k$. Helper `apply_controlled_paulis` mengimplementasikan faktor tensor tetapi mengabaikan fase itu. Di bawah kontrol, fase itu menjadi fase relatif pada ancilla, jadi kita memulihkannya dengan $S^\\dagger$, $Z$, atau $S$ untuk eksponen 1, 2, atau 3. Untuk pasangan CPC, gunakan `L.phase + R.phase` modulo empat. Contoh satu sisi di bawah hanya memakai `L.phase`. Lihat [Martiel & Javadi-Abhari, Supplementary Information §II-C dan Fig. S3](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725).\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"**One-sided checks**\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A CPC pair can also compare an ancilla result with the final data bits. As in [van den Berg et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03937), implement $L = U^\\dagger R U$ with controlled gates and evaluate $R$ classically when it is diagonal in the measurement basis. For the $R=Z_8Z_9$ example from [Section 2.2](#coherent), the syndrome is the input-check bit XOR the two output bits. Ideal shots give zero, while an $X$ fault on qubit 8 after the payload flips that syndrome.\n"
msgstr "Pasangan CPC juga bisa membandingkan hasil ancilla dengan bit data akhir. Seperti di [van den Berg et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03937), implementasikan $L = U^\\dagger R U$ dengan gate terkontrol dan evaluasi $R$ secara klasik saat ia diagonal dalam basis pengukuran. Untuk contoh $R=Z_8Z_9$ dari [Bagian 2.2](#coherent), syndrome-nya adalah bit check input XOR kedua bit output. Shot ideal memberi nol, sedangkan error $X$ pada qubit 8 setelah payload membalik syndrome itu.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The cell below reuses `apply_controlled_paulis` on the same payload, with no inverse and no controlled-$R$ gates. This preserves the ideal final Z-basis statistics, although measuring $L$ can change the underlying data state. It illustrates the check identity, rather than benchmarking its hardware benefit.\n"
msgstr "Sel di bawah memakai ulang `apply_controlled_paulis` pada payload yang sama, tanpa invers dan tanpa gate controlled-$R$. Ini mempertahankan statistik Z-basis akhir yang ideal, meskipun pengukuran $L$ bisa mengubah state data yang mendasarinya. Ini menggambarkan identitas check, bukan membandingkan manfaat hardware-nya.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Measuring $L$ on an ancilla is a projective measurement of the payload. Unless $L$ is a stabilizer of the state at that point, the measurement collapses the state onto a random $\\pm 1$ eigenspace of $L$. The eigenvalue is then faithfully carried to $R$ (which is why the XOR still passes), but the payload that continues through the circuit is no longer the state the computation prepared. In this one-sided construction, the data state can change while the ideal statistics in the chosen measurement basis remain unchanged.\n"
msgstr "Mengukur $L$ pada sebuah ancilla adalah pengukuran proyektif terhadap payload. Kecuali $L$ adalah stabilizer dari state pada titik itu, pengukuran meruntuhkan state ke ruang eigen $\\pm 1$ acak dari $L$. Nilai eigen itu lalu terbawa dengan setia ke $R$ (itulah sebabnya XOR tetap lolos), tetapi payload yang melanjutkan Circuit tidak lagi state yang disiapkan komputasi. Dalam konstruksi satu sisi ini, state data bisa berubah sementara statistik ideal pada basis pengukuran yang dipilih tetap sama.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Section 2.3.3 takes a different simplification of the CPC identity, as follows: choose $L$ to be a known input stabilizer, so its physical check can be omitted. This leads to an interior stabilizer check.\n"
msgstr "Bagian 2.3.3 mengambil penyederhanaan lain dari identitas CPC, sebagai berikut: pilih $L$ sebagai stabilizer input yang diketahui, sehingga check fisiknya bisa dihilangkan. Ini mengarah ke check stabilizer interior.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.3.3 From CPC to spacetime codes: placing checks where they work {#233-from-cpc-to-spacetime-codes-placing-checks-where-they-work}"
msgstr "2.3.3 Dari CPC ke spacetime code: menempatkan check di tempat ia bekerja {#233-from-cpc-to-spacetime-codes-placing-checks-where-they-work}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Two-sided CPC compares $L$ at the input with $R$ at the output. If $L$ is a known input stabilizer, its value supplies the reference and its physical check can be omitted, leaving a right-only check. The input $|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}$ is stabilized by any product of $Z$ operators, such as $Z_j$ or the all-$Z$ operator. Choose $L=Z_j$ and propagate it forward through the payload:\n"
msgstr "CPC dua sisi membandingkan $L$ di input dengan $R$ di output. Jika $L$ adalah stabilizer input yang diketahui, nilainya menyediakan referensi dan check fisiknya bisa dihilangkan, menyisakan check right-only. Input $|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}$ distabilkan oleh hasil kali operator $Z$ apa pun, seperti $Z_j$ atau operator all-$Z$. Pilih $L=Z_j$ dan rambatkan maju melalui payload:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$R_j=U Z_j U^\\dagger,\\qquad R_j\\, U|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}=U|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}.$$\n"
msgstr "$$R_j=U Z_j U^\\dagger,\\qquad R_j\\, U|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}=U|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}.$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Measuring $R_j$ at the output therefore gives $+1$ ideally. An arbitrary CPC choice of $R$ need not have a known value, so dropping its partner can produce random outcomes. Clifford propagation keeps each $R_j$ a single Pauli, and the circuit sets its type and support. Finding $R_j$ means computing classically what the payload does to $Z_j$: efficient at any depth for our Clifford payload, but in a general circuit with non-Clifford gates typically cheap only through the first layers. A right-only check therefore gives a general computation a solid start, not a way around classical simulation.\n"
msgstr "Mengukur $R_j$ di output karenanya memberi $+1$ secara ideal. Pilihan $R$ CPC sembarang belum tentu punya nilai yang diketahui, jadi membuang pasangannya bisa menghasilkan hasil acak. Perambatan Clifford menjaga setiap $R_j$ tetap satu Pauli, dan Circuit menentukan tipe serta support-nya. Menemukan $R_j$ berarti menghitung secara klasik apa yang dilakukan payload pada $Z_j$: efisien pada kedalaman berapa pun untuk payload Clifford kita, tetapi pada Circuit umum dengan gate non-Clifford biasanya murah hanya melewati beberapa lapisan pertama. Jadi check right-only memberi komputasi umum awal yang kokoh, bukan cara menghindari simulasi klasik.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-5}"
msgstr "Cek pemahamanmu {#check-your-understanding-5}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Why not measure an arbitrary CPC pair $L$ and $R$ separately, then XOR their outcomes?\n"
msgstr "Mengapa tidak mengukur pasangan CPC sembarang $L$ dan $R$ secara terpisah, lalu meng-XOR hasilnya?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The XOR agrees ideally, but measuring $L$ can project the data onto a random eigenstate and disrupt later coherent computation. The coherent CPC sandwich avoids revealing either individual value. Measuring a known stabilizer is harmless ideally, while reconstructing a partner from compatible final data measurements, as above, preserves those measurement statistics.\n"
msgstr "XOR-nya sepakat secara ideal, tetapi mengukur $L$ bisa memproyeksikan data ke eigenstate acak dan mengganggu komputasi koheren selanjutnya. Sandwich CPC yang koheren menghindari terungkapnya nilai masing-masing. Mengukur stabilizer yang diketahui tidak berbahaya secara ideal, sedangkan merekonstruksi pasangan dari pengukuran data akhir yang kompatibel, seperti di atas, mempertahankan statistik pengukuran tersebut.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"For each $R_j$, prepare an ancilla in $|+\\rangle$, apply its controlled-Pauli factors and any sign correction, then measure in the $X$ basis (H followed by a computational-basis measurement). The ideal result is bit `0`, corresponding to eigenvalue $+1$.\n"
msgstr ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"Untuk tiap $R_j$, siapkan ancilla dalam $|+\\rangle$, terapkan faktor controlled-Pauli-nya dan koreksi tanda apa pun, lalu ukur dalam basis $X$ (H diikuti pengukuran basis komputasi). Hasil idealnya adalah bit `0`, yang sesuai dengan nilai eigen $+1$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The next cell measures all 20 generators to illustrate the routing cost; using a subset also gives valid right-only checks. The 20 data qubits and 20 ancillas sit on one line, as on a device with only nearest-neighbor couplers. Each ancilla must meet every data qubit in the support of its $R_j$, so the ancillas are swapped through the data line. A controlled-Pauli fused with the swap that follows it costs two CZ gates [van den Berg et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03937), and a swap past a data qubit outside the support still costs three. The cell checks that no ancilla fires without noise, compares CZ counts, and draws the circuit. The payload sits left of the barrier, and the diamond of SWAP gates carries the ancillas up through the data line.\n"
msgstr "Sel berikutnya mengukur semua 20 generator untuk mengilustrasikan biaya routing; memakai subset juga memberi check right-only yang valid. Ke-20 qubit data dan 20 ancilla berada di satu garis, seperti pada perangkat dengan coupler tetangga-terdekat saja. Tiap ancilla harus bertemu setiap qubit data dalam support $R_j$-nya, jadi ancilla di-swap melewati garis data. Sebuah controlled-Pauli yang digabung dengan swap setelahnya berharga dua gate CZ [van den Berg et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03937), dan swap melewati qubit data di luar support tetap berharga tiga. Sel ini memeriksa bahwa tidak ada ancilla yang menyala tanpa noise, membandingkan jumlah CZ, dan menggambar Circuit. Payload ada di kiri barrier, dan berlian gate SWAP membawa ancilla naik melewati garis data.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i/extracted-outputs/4c2e2a1e-1.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i/extracted-outputs/4c2e2a1e-1.avif)\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every check in this circuit is valid, and each ancilla measures one stabilizer, so each catches the faults that anti-commute with its $R_j$. The cost is the problem. In this layout, reaching the supports takes 400 swaps, and even with each controlled-Pauli fused into a swap, the checks need more than twenty times as many CZ gates as the payload they protect. Those gates and the waiting ancillas add errors of their own, so on hardware the added noise can easily outweigh what the checks detect. These counts describe this routing construction, not a minimum: placement and check support determine the routing cost on the nearest-neighbor line.\n"
"A check need not occupy one time slice. A *wire* $w$ is a qubit between two layers. Let $A_w$ be the circuit prefix up to that wire. The Pauli's back-propagator is $B(P,w)=A_w^\\dagger P A_w$. For input $|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}$, couplings in time order $1,\\ldots,m$ form a valid check when:\n"
msgstr ""
"Setiap check di Circuit ini valid, dan tiap ancilla mengukur satu stabilizer, jadi masing-masing menangkap error yang anti-komut dengan $R_j$-nya. Masalahnya ada di biaya. Pada tata letak ini, menjangkau support butuh 400 swap, dan bahkan dengan tiap controlled-Pauli digabung ke dalam swap, check membutuhkan lebih dari dua puluh kali lipat gate CZ dibanding payload yang dilindunginya. Gate-gate itu dan ancilla yang menunggu menambah error mereka sendiri, jadi di hardware noise tambahan bisa dengan mudah melebihi apa yang dideteksi check. Jumlah ini menggambarkan konstruksi routing ini, bukan minimum: penempatan dan support check menentukan biaya routing pada garis tetangga-terdekat.\n"
"Sebuah check tidak harus menempati satu irisan waktu. Sebuah *wire* $w$ adalah qubit di antara dua layer. Misalkan $A_w$ adalah prefiks Circuit sampai wire itu. Back-propagator Pauli adalah $B(P,w)=A_w^\\dagger P A_w$. Untuk input $|0\\rangle^{\\otimes n}$, kopling dalam urutan waktu $1,\\ldots,m$ membentuk check yang valid ketika:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$B(P_m,w_m)\\cdots B(P_1,w_1)=i^\\phi S,\\qquad S\\in\\langle Z_0,\\ldots,Z_{n-1}\\rangle.$$\n"
msgstr "$$B(P_m,w_m)\\cdots B(P_1,w_1)=i^\\phi S,\\qquad S\\in\\langle Z_0,\\ldots,Z_{n-1}\\rangle.$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Prepare the ancilla in $|+\\rangle$, apply each controlled Pauli at its wire, cancel the accumulated phase $i^\\phi$ on the ancilla, and measure it in the $X$ basis ([Martiel & Javadi-Abhari, Eq. 2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725)). We keep every coupling on one neighboring data qubit to avoid routing while allowing support at different times.\n"
"<span id=\"paulice\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""
"Siapkan ancilla dalam $|+\\rangle$, terapkan tiap controlled Pauli pada wire-nya, batalkan fase terakumulasi $i^\\phi$ pada ancilla, dan ukur dalam basis $X$ ([Martiel & Javadi-Abhari, Pers. 2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.15725)). Kita jaga setiap kopling pada satu qubit data tetangga untuk menghindari routing sambil tetap membolehkan support pada waktu berbeda.\n"
"<span id=\"paulice\"></span>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "2.4 Spacetime checks with Paulice {#24-spacetime-checks-with-paulice}"
msgstr "2.4 Check spacetime dengan Paulice {#24-spacetime-checks-with-paulice}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The right-only example in Section 2.3.3 showed that removing $L$ does not eliminate routing. Each ancilla still has to reach the support of its output stabilizer. The local spacetime construction introduced above avoids this routing by placing an ancilla's couplings at different times on a single neighboring data wire.\n"
msgstr "Contoh right-only di Bagian 2.3.3 menunjukkan bahwa menghilangkan $L$ tidak menghilangkan routing. Tiap ancilla tetap harus menjangkau support stabilizer outputnya. Konstruksi spacetime lokal yang diperkenalkan di atas menghindari routing ini dengan menempatkan kopling sebuah ancilla pada waktu berbeda di satu wire data tetangga.\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "[Qiskit Paulice](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/addons/qiskit-addon-paulice) automates this search for a given circuit and measurement. Each data qubit with a free neighbor on the device can be a *target*, and that neighbor becomes its check ancilla. With `method=\"windowed\"`, as in the [IBM Quantum spacetime-code tutorial](/tutorials/spacetime-codes), `add_pauli_checks` searches windows of couplings along each target's wire. A check's syndrome is the XOR of its ancilla bit and, where the search needs them, final data bits listed in `check_support`. Data bits are read out anyway, so that part of a check costs no gates.\n"
msgstr "[Qiskit Paulice](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/addons/qiskit-addon-paulice) mengotomatiskan pencarian ini untuk Circuit dan pengukuran tertentu. Setiap qubit data yang punya tetangga bebas di perangkat bisa menjadi *target*, dan tetangga itu menjadi ancilla check-nya. Dengan `method=\"windowed\"`, seperti di [tutorial spacetime-code IBM Quantum](/tutorials/spacetime-codes), `add_pauli_checks` mencari jendela kopling sepanjang wire tiap target. Syndrome sebuah check adalah XOR dari bit ancilla-nya dan, jika pencarian membutuhkannya, bit data akhir yang tercantum di `check_support`. Bit data tetap dibaca, jadi bagian check itu tidak butuh gate.\n"

# doq: fixed after review 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Paulice scores checks with *gamma*: under a noise model built from the device calibration, it estimates the quasi-probability cost of cancelling the noise the checks leave undetected within the lightcone of the measurement, and lower is better. Gamma is a model score, distinct from the acceptance overhead $1/A$ of Part 1. Checks are committed one target at a time, and the search returns the circuit after each commitment. The model takes the error of each data-line coupler from the calibration but gives the couplers a check adds a typical rate, so it would not notice a faulty one; we therefore keep only target-ancilla pairs with calibrated coupler error below 0.05 and ancilla readout error below 0.1.\n"
msgstr "Paulice menilai check dengan *gamma*: di bawah model noise yang dibangun dari kalibrasi perangkat, ia memperkirakan biaya quasi-probabilitas untuk membatalkan noise yang tidak terdeteksi check dalam lightcone pengukuran, dan makin rendah makin baik. Gamma adalah skor model, berbeda dari overhead penerimaan $1/A$ di Bagian 1. Check dikomit satu target pada satu waktu, dan pencarian mengembalikan Circuit setelah tiap komitmen. Model mengambil error tiap coupler garis-data dari kalibrasi tetapi memberi coupler yang ditambahkan check sebuah laju tipikal, jadi ia tidak akan menyadari yang rusak; karena itu kita hanya menyimpan pasangan target-ancilla dengan error coupler terkalibrasi di bawah 0.05 dan readout error ancilla di bawah 0.1.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For Sections [2.4](#paulice)-[2.5](#hardware-experiment), we extend the same 20-qubit circuit construction from five to ten CZ layers, keeping the generator and random seed fixed. The deeper payload gives errors more opportunities to accumulate and spread, so that we can test whether post-selection can outweigh the added check cost. We run this payload forward, without its inverse, and sample ten signed stabilizers of its ideal output. The stabilizers and Paulice checks are computed for this deeper circuit. We select one physical data line and reuse it for every unchecked and checked circuit. The first sampled stabilizer $G_0$ supplies the measurement for the placement comparison below, and [Section 2.5](#hardware-experiment) repeats the search for each stabilizer. We first recall how a check's support moves backward through the gates.\n"
"The idea is to take a check operator and pull it backward through the circuit, one gate at a time. At each layer, we record which qubits the operator touches; together, these qubits and layers form the check's detection region. To do this, we need to know how Pauli operators transform when they pass through a gate in reverse. For any Clifford gate, the result is always another Pauli, which is what makes the tracking exact. Our payload uses CZ gates and single-qubit Clifford gates. A CZ, for example, maps:\n"
msgstr ""
"Untuk Bagian [2.4](#paulice)-[2.5](#hardware-experiment), kita memperluas konstruksi circuit 20-qubit yang sama dari lima menjadi sepuluh layer CZ, dengan generator dan random seed tetap sama. Payload yang lebih dalam ini memberi error lebih banyak kesempatan untuk menumpuk dan menyebar, sehingga kita bisa menguji apakah post-selection bisa mengimbangi biaya tambahan dari check. Kita menjalankan payload ini ke arah maju, tanpa inversnya, dan mengambil sampel sepuluh stabilizer bertanda dari output idealnya. Stabilizer dan check Paulice dihitung untuk circuit yang lebih dalam ini. Kita memilih satu data line fisik dan memakainya ulang untuk setiap circuit tanpa check maupun dengan check. Stabilizer pertama yang disampel, $G_0$, menyediakan pengukuran untuk perbandingan penempatan di bawah, dan [Bagian 2.5](#hardware-experiment) mengulang pencarian untuk tiap stabilizer. Pertama-tama kita ingat dulu bagaimana support sebuah check bergerak mundur melewati gate.\n"
"Idenya adalah mengambil sebuah operator check lalu menariknya mundur melewati circuit, satu gate demi satu gate. Di setiap layer, kita mencatat qubit mana saja yang disentuh operator tersebut; qubit dan layer ini bersama-sama membentuk detection region dari check. Untuk itu, kita perlu tahu bagaimana operator Pauli berubah saat melewati sebuah gate secara terbalik. Untuk gate Clifford apa pun, hasilnya selalu berupa Pauli lain, dan itulah yang membuat pelacakannya eksak. Payload kita memakai gate CZ dan gate Clifford satu-qubit. Sebuah CZ, misalnya, memetakan:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$X_a \\xrightarrow{\\text{CZ}(a, b)} X_a Z_b,$$\n"
msgstr "$$X_a \\xrightarrow{\\text{CZ}(a, b)} X_a Z_b,$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "so an $X$ in a check's support picks up a $Z$ on the partner qubit, while a $Z$ passes through unchanged. Each two-qubit layer can add qubits to the support or remove them, so the detection region changes shape as it moves backward in time. The rules for CNOT and CZ are collected below.\n"
msgstr "jadi sebuah $X$ di support sebuah check akan mendapat $Z$ pada qubit pasangannya, sedangkan $Z$ lewat tanpa berubah. Setiap layer dua-qubit bisa menambah atau mengurangi qubit di support, jadi detection region berubah bentuk saat bergerak mundur dalam waktu. Aturan untuk CNOT dan CZ dirangkum di bawah.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Conjugation rules for two-qubit gates, and what single-qubit gates do\">\n"
msgstr ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Aturan konjugasi untuk gate dua-qubit, dan apa yang dilakukan gate satu-qubit\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The conjugation $U^\\dagger P U$ pulls a check operator $P$ at the output of a gate back to its input. Forward propagation of an error instead uses $U P U^\\dagger$. For Clifford gates, the result is always another Pauli.\n"
msgstr "Konjugasi $U^\\dagger P U$ menarik operator check $P$ di output sebuah gate kembali ke inputnya. Propagasi error ke arah maju justru memakai $U P U^\\dagger$. Untuk gate Clifford, hasilnya selalu berupa Pauli lain.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Single-qubit gates (H, S, and so on) can change the type of error on a wire (for example, $X \\leftrightarrow Z$ through H) but cannot spread it to another qubit. Only two-qubit entangling gates move errors between wires, which is why detection regions grow only at entangling layers.\n"
msgstr "Gate satu-qubit (H, S, dan seterusnya) bisa mengubah jenis error pada sebuah wire (misalnya, $X \\leftrightarrow Z$ lewat H) tetapi tidak bisa menyebarkannya ke qubit lain. Hanya gate entangling dua-qubit yang memindahkan error antar wire, itulah sebabnya detection region hanya membesar di layer entangling.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*CNOT* (control $c$, target $t$), an *asymmetric* gate:\n"
msgstr "*CNOT* (control $c$, target $t$), gate yang *asimetris*:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$X_c \\mapsto X_c X_t, \\quad Z_c \\mapsto Z_c, \\quad X_t \\mapsto X_t, \\quad Z_t \\mapsto Z_c Z_t.$$\n"
msgstr "$$X_c \\mapsto X_c X_t, \\quad Z_c \\mapsto Z_c, \\quad X_t \\mapsto X_t, \\quad Z_t \\mapsto Z_c Z_t.$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "CNOT does \"if control is 1, flip target.\" This asymmetry determines how different errors propagate:\n"
msgstr "CNOT melakukan \"jika control bernilai 1, balik target.\" Asimetri ini menentukan bagaimana berbagai error merambat:\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Bit-flips ($X$) travel *control → target*. An $X$ on the control changes whether the target gets flipped, so the error spreads to the target ($X_c \\mapsto X_c X_t$). An $X$ on the target flips it regardless of the control, so it stays put ($X_t \\mapsto X_t$).\n"
msgstr "Bit-flip ($X$) merambat *control → target*. Sebuah $X$ pada control mengubah apakah target dibalik atau tidak, jadi error menyebar ke target ($X_c \\mapsto X_c X_t$). Sebuah $X$ pada target membaliknya apa pun kondisi control, jadi ia tetap di tempat ($X_t \\mapsto X_t$).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Phase-flips ($Z$) travel *target → control*. CNOT makes the target's value depend on the control ($|c,t\\rangle \\to |c, c \\oplus t\\rangle$), so a phase applied to the target effectively becomes conditional on the control's value, kicking a phase back to the control. This is the same *phase kickback* mechanism used in algorithms like Deutsch-Jozsa and quantum phase estimation. The result is $Z_t \\mapsto Z_c Z_t$. Meanwhile $Z$ on the control does not involve the target at all, so $Z_c \\mapsto Z_c$.\n"
msgstr "Phase-flip ($Z$) merambat *target → control*. CNOT membuat nilai target bergantung pada control ($|c,t\\rangle \\to |c, c \\oplus t\\rangle$), jadi fase yang diterapkan pada target secara efektif menjadi bersyarat pada nilai control, sehingga fase \"menendang balik\" ke control. Ini adalah mekanisme *phase kickback* yang sama seperti yang dipakai di algoritma seperti Deutsch-Jozsa dan quantum phase estimation. Hasilnya adalah $Z_t \\mapsto Z_c Z_t$. Sementara itu $Z$ pada control sama sekali tidak melibatkan target, jadi $Z_c \\mapsto Z_c$.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In short: *bit-flips flow forward* (control → target), *phase-flips flow backward* (target → control). This directional asymmetry is why backward propagation of $Z$-type checks through a CNOT chain fans out from target toward controls.\n"
msgstr "Singkatnya: *bit-flip mengalir maju* (control → target), *phase-flip mengalir mundur* (target → control). Asimetri arah ini menjelaskan kenapa propagasi mundur check tipe-$Z$ melewati rantai CNOT menyebar dari target ke arah control.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "*CZ* (qubits $a$, $b$), a *symmetric* gate:\n"
msgstr "*CZ* (qubit $a$, $b$), gate yang *simetris*:\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "$$X_a \\mapsto X_a Z_b, \\quad Z_a \\mapsto Z_a, \\quad X_b \\mapsto Z_a X_b, \\quad Z_b \\mapsto Z_b.$$\n"
msgstr "$$X_a \\mapsto X_a Z_b, \\quad Z_a \\mapsto Z_a, \\quad X_b \\mapsto Z_a X_b, \\quad Z_b \\mapsto Z_b.$$\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unlike CNOT, CZ treats both qubits identically; there is no \"control\" or \"target.\" A bit-flip on either qubit picks up a phase-flip on the other. Phase-flips pass through unchanged on both sides. The symmetry means error propagation looks the same from either direction.\n"
msgstr "Berbeda dengan CNOT, CZ memperlakukan kedua qubit secara identik; tidak ada \"control\" atau \"target.\" Bit-flip pada salah satu qubit mendapat phase-flip pada qubit lainnya. Phase-flip lewat tanpa berubah di kedua sisi. Simetri ini berarti perambatan error tampak sama dari arah mana pun.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The pattern to remember: entangling gates *spread* errors. A single-qubit error before the gate can become a two-qubit error after it. This is both the source of the problem (errors propagate through the circuit) and the basis of detection (a check's sensitivity propagates the same way).\n"
msgstr "Pola yang perlu diingat: gate entangling *menyebarkan* error. Error satu-qubit sebelum gate bisa menjadi error dua-qubit sesudahnya. Ini sekaligus sumber masalahnya (error merambat lewat circuit) dan dasar dari deteksi (sensitivitas sebuah check merambat dengan cara yang sama).\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i/extracted-outputs/bed76e8d-1.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/learning/images/courses/tools-for-quantum-advantage/error-correction-i/extracted-outputs/bed76e8d-1.avif)\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The first table lists one check per target. Each check couples its ancilla to the target wire at a few points along the payload, the layers shown in the table, and its syndrome might add final data bits. The second table follows the search as it commits checks. Compare the change in gamma and the added two-qubit gates at each step. The targets are ordered by their single-check scores, but overlapping detection regions and noise from added gates mean that the largest improvement need not come first, and a committed check can raise gamma. The device map shows data qubits in green, committed check ancillas in orange, and unused qubits in gray. Each check ancilla is next to its target, so the couplings need no routing.\n"
"Each check covers the spacetime region its couplings and syndrome bits reach by back-propagation, as in Section 2.3.3. Coverage is not the whole story, however. Each check adds gates, and gamma weighs the detected noise against the noise those gates bring. Gamma depends on the noise model and on the chosen measurement, so a lower gamma is a search preference, not a guarantee of a better hardware result, and it is not an acceptance rate or a measured fidelity.\n"
msgstr ""
"Tabel pertama mencantumkan satu check per target. Tiap check menghubungkan ancilla-nya ke wire target di beberapa titik sepanjang payload, yaitu layer yang ditampilkan di tabel, dan syndrome-nya mungkin menambah bit data di akhir. Tabel kedua mengikuti pencarian saat ia meng-commit check. Bandingkan perubahan gamma dan gate dua-qubit tambahan di setiap langkah. Target diurutkan berdasarkan skor single-check-nya, tetapi detection region yang tumpang tindih dan noise dari gate tambahan berarti peningkatan terbesar belum tentu datang lebih dulu, dan check yang sudah di-commit bisa menaikkan gamma. Peta device menampilkan data qubit dengan warna hijau, ancilla check yang di-commit dengan oranye, dan qubit yang tidak dipakai dengan abu-abu. Tiap ancilla check berada di sebelah targetnya, jadi kopling tidak memerlukan routing.\n"
"Tiap check mencakup region spacetime yang dijangkau kopling dan bit syndrome-nya lewat back-propagation, seperti di Bagian 2.3.3. Namun cakupan bukan satu-satunya hal yang penting. Tiap check menambah gate, dan gamma menimbang noise yang terdeteksi terhadap noise yang dibawa gate tersebut. Gamma bergantung pada model noise dan pada pengukuran yang dipilih, jadi gamma yang lebih rendah adalah preferensi pencarian, bukan jaminan hasil hardware yang lebih baik, dan ia bukan acceptance rate atau fidelity yang terukur.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The checks also depend on the measured observable. Here they were chosen for $G_0$. [Section 2.5](#hardware-experiment) repeats the search for every sampled stabilizer and runs the resulting circuits on hardware.\n"
msgstr "Check juga bergantung pada observable yang diukur. Di sini check dipilih untuk $G_0$. [Bagian 2.5](#hardware-experiment) mengulang pencarian untuk setiap stabilizer yang disampel dan menjalankan circuit hasilnya di hardware.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding-6}"
msgstr "Cek pemahamanmu {#check-your-understanding-6}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A check has a detection region covering some set of spacetime locations. Does this mean it catches every error at those locations?\n"
msgstr "Sebuah check punya detection region yang mencakup sekumpulan lokasi spacetime. Apakah ini berarti ia menangkap setiap error di lokasi-lokasi itu?\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-10-13 · verified 2026-10-22
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Not every type. At each location in the detection region, the check detects errors that anti-commute with its propagated support there; errors that commute pass through undetected. For one check, the propagated support at a location is a single Pauli, so the check catches two of $X$, $Y$, and $Z$ there and misses the third. This is why a single check has weaknesses even within its own detection region, and why complementary checks with different Pauli types or placements are needed for broader protection.\n"
msgstr "Tidak semua jenis. Di setiap lokasi dalam detection region, check mendeteksi error yang anti-commute dengan support-nya yang dipropagasi di sana; error yang commute lolos tanpa terdeteksi. Untuk satu check, support yang dipropagasi di suatu lokasi adalah satu Pauli, jadi check menangkap dua dari $X$, $Y$, dan $Z$ di sana dan melewatkan yang ketiga. Inilah alasan satu check punya kelemahan bahkan di dalam detection region-nya sendiri, dan kenapa check yang saling melengkapi dengan jenis Pauli atau penempatan berbeda diperlukan untuk perlindungan yang lebih luas.\n"

